WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.115
Here's a variable, X.

00:00:02.115 --> 00:00:03.411
We put 2 in it.

00:00:03.411 --> 00:00:05.140
Then we put 4 in it.

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:07.505
Print X, and you get 4.

00:00:07.505 --> 00:00:08.732
The 2 is gone.

00:00:08.732 --> 00:00:12.850
A variable like this holds one value at a time.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.360
But what if you need to keep 100 exam scores, or every word in a book?

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:24.470
Then you need a collection: many values, carried around under one name.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.598
Python has three main collections.

00:00:27.598 --> 00:00:31.847
A list keeps values in order, and you can change it.

00:00:31.847 --> 00:00:35.279
A dictionary labels each value with a key.

00:00:35.279 --> 00:00:40.100
And a tuple keeps values in order, but you can't change it.

00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:47.227
Welcome to Lecture 5 of Introduction to AI Programming: lists, dictionaries and tuples.

00:00:47.577 --> 00:00:54.337
This first part is all about lists: how to write one, loop over it, look inside it, and change it.

00:00:55.134 --> 00:01:00.494
A list is written with square brackets, and its elements are separated by commas.

00:01:00.844 --> 00:01:05.044
The elements can be numbers, or strings, or a mix of both.

00:01:05.394 --> 00:01:08.662
An element can even be another list.

00:01:08.662 --> 00:01:12.474
Here, the middle element is the list 5, 6.

00:01:12.824 --> 00:01:16.184
And a list can be empty: just two brackets.

00:01:16.967 --> 00:01:19.900
Lists and for loops are best friends.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:25.447
Here's a list of three friends: Tom, then Jerry, and last of all, Bat.

00:01:25.797 --> 00:01:30.437
The for loop runs its body once for each element, in order.

00:01:30.787 --> 00:01:35.955
On each pass, the loop variable, friend, refers to the next element.

00:01:35.955 --> 00:01:38.387
First Tom, then Jerry, then Bat.

00:01:38.737 --> 00:01:42.897
After the last element, the loop ends, and Python prints Done.

00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:46.521
Just like a string, a list is a sequence.

00:01:46.521 --> 00:01:50.627
Each element has an index: its position, counted from zero.

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:52.702
So friends[1] is Glenn.

00:01:52.702 --> 00:01:55.777
That's the second element, not the first.

00:01:56.127 --> 00:01:58.587
A negative index counts from the end.

00:01:58.587 --> 00:02:01.047
Minus one is the last element, Sally.

00:02:01.397 --> 00:02:08.117
Ask for index three, and Python stops with an IndexError: list index out of range.

00:02:08.901 --> 00:02:11.117
Pause the video and predict.

00:02:11.117 --> 00:02:16.261
A list has N elements. What are its first and last valid indices?

00:02:16.811 --> 00:02:20.621
The first is zero, and the last is N minus one.

00:02:20.621 --> 00:02:25.971
Asking for index N is the most common off-by-one error with lists.

00:02:26.768 --> 00:02:31.599
Strings are immutable: you can't change a character inside a string.

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:36.288
Try to change the first letter of Banana, and you get a TypeError.

00:02:36.638 --> 00:02:39.239
You can only make a new string.

00:02:39.239 --> 00:02:45.198
fruit.lower() makes a new string, 'banana', and leaves fruit as it was.

00:02:45.548 --> 00:02:49.563
Lists are different. Lists are mutable.

00:02:49.563 --> 00:02:55.948
lotto[2] = 28 changes the element at index two, from 26 to 28.

00:02:56.298 --> 00:02:57.913
No new list is made.

00:02:57.913 --> 00:03:00.176
The list itself has changed.

00:03:00.176 --> 00:03:04.458
Hold on to that difference: it's the key to part two.

00:03:05.235 --> 00:03:09.899
The built-in function len() gives the number of elements in a sequence.

00:03:09.899 --> 00:03:13.249
For the string 'Hello Bob', that's nine characters.

00:03:13.249 --> 00:03:15.155
For this list, four elements.

00:03:15.505 --> 00:03:20.511
range(4) stands for the numbers zero, one, two and three.

00:03:20.511 --> 00:03:24.025
The stop value, four, is never included.

00:03:24.375 --> 00:03:28.765
With three arguments, the third is the step.

00:03:28.765 --> 00:03:33.455
range(2, 10, 2) gives two, four, six and eight.

00:03:33.805 --> 00:03:36.161
One correction to the slides.

00:03:36.161 --> 00:03:39.656
In Python 3, range() doesn't return a list.

00:03:39.656 --> 00:03:45.426
It returns a range object, which works out each number when it's asked.

00:03:45.426 --> 00:03:49.165
If you need a real list, write list(range(4)).

00:03:49.935 --> 00:03:52.895
Now we can write the same loop in two ways.

00:03:53.245 --> 00:03:55.605
The first loops over the elements.

00:03:55.605 --> 00:04:01.159
The second loops over the indices, zero, one and two, and looks each element up.

00:04:01.159 --> 00:04:02.965
They print the same lines.

00:04:03.315 --> 00:04:04.455
Prefer the first.

00:04:04.455 --> 00:04:07.675
It's shorter, and there's no index to get wrong.

00:04:08.025 --> 00:04:13.262
Use the index loop when you need the position, for example to change the elements.

00:04:13.262 --> 00:04:15.945
This loop doubles every price in the list.

00:04:16.295 --> 00:04:17.279
But watch out.

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:20.584
This loop looks like it doubles the prices too.

00:04:20.584 --> 00:04:22.975
It doesn't. The list is unchanged.

00:04:23.325 --> 00:04:24.316
Here's why.

00:04:24.316 --> 00:04:28.643
On each pass, the name `p` refers to an element.

00:04:28.643 --> 00:04:34.141
`p = p * 2` makes a new number, and moves the name `p` to it.

00:04:34.141 --> 00:04:36.485
The list is never touched.

00:04:37.268 --> 00:04:39.567
Two more ways to make new lists.

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:42.728
Like strings, lists can be joined with plus.

00:04:42.728 --> 00:04:47.828
The result is a new list, and the lists you started with are unchanged.

00:04:48.178 --> 00:04:50.916
A slice takes part of a list.

00:04:50.916 --> 00:04:56.393
T[1:3] gives the elements at index one and two: 41 and 12.

00:04:56.393 --> 00:04:59.698
Up to, but not including, the stop.

00:05:00.048 --> 00:05:02.891
Leave out the start to begin at the start.

00:05:02.891 --> 00:05:05.328
Leave out the stop to go to the end.

00:05:05.678 --> 00:05:08.740
Leave out both, and you get a copy of the whole list.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:10.358
We'll need that in part two.

00:05:10.708 --> 00:05:14.786
And a slice never raises an IndexError.

00:05:14.786 --> 00:05:18.028
T[4:100] just stops at the end.

00:05:18.801 --> 00:05:24.847
A list is an object with methods: functions that belong to it, called with a dot.

00:05:24.847 --> 00:05:27.161
These are all the list methods.

00:05:27.511 --> 00:05:33.921
To build a list from scratch, start with an empty list, and append elements one at a time.

00:05:33.921 --> 00:05:36.271
Each new element goes on the end.

00:05:36.621 --> 00:05:39.664
`insert` puts an element at a given index.

00:05:39.664 --> 00:05:42.997
`pop` removes the last element and returns it.

00:05:42.997 --> 00:05:47.126
`remove` deletes the first element equal to its argument.

00:05:47.126 --> 00:05:50.821
And `extend` appends every element of another list.

00:05:51.171 --> 00:05:54.105
Each of these changes the list it's called on.

00:05:54.105 --> 00:05:57.996
Start with an empty list before a loop, and append inside it.

00:05:57.996 --> 00:06:00.611
That's the accumulate pattern, for lists.

00:06:00.961 --> 00:06:05.321
The `in` operator asks whether a value is in a list.

00:06:05.321 --> 00:06:09.010
Nine is in this list, so the answer is True.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:11.441
Fifteen isn't, so it's False.

00:06:12.235 --> 00:06:16.429
A list keeps the order you give it, until you change the order.

00:06:16.429 --> 00:06:22.355
The `sort` method sorts the list in place: strings in dictionary order, numbers by value.

00:06:22.705 --> 00:06:24.706
But `sort` returns None.

00:06:24.706 --> 00:06:30.543
So `x = x.sort()` throws your list away, and leaves `x` equal to None.

00:06:30.543 --> 00:06:38.465
If you want a sorted copy, use the built-in function `sorted()`, which we'll meet in part four.

00:06:39.065 --> 00:06:45.038
Built-in functions also take lists: `len()`, `max()`, `min()` and `sum()`.

00:06:45.038 --> 00:06:48.025
Sum divided by len gives the average.

00:06:48.375 --> 00:06:54.975
So an averaging program can simply store the numbers in a list, and use `sum()` and `len()` at the end.

00:06:55.768 --> 00:06:56.928
Let's sum up.

00:06:57.278 --> 00:07:00.676
A list is an ordered sequence you can change.

00:07:00.676 --> 00:07:05.358
Index from zero, and slice up to, but not including, the stop.

00:07:05.708 --> 00:07:11.788
Methods like `append` and `sort` change the list itself, and mostly return None.

00:07:12.138 --> 00:07:16.658
Plus and slicing build new lists, and leave the old ones alone.

00:07:17.008 --> 00:07:18.276
Here's one to try.

00:07:18.276 --> 00:07:26.728
Write a function, `evens`, that returns a new list of the even numbers in a list, without changing the list you pass in.

00:07:27.278 --> 00:07:32.703
In part two, we'll see what really happens when two names refer to the same list.

00:07:32.703 --> 00:07:35.918
It's the most important picture in this lecture.

